  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 246 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Ridging will become more established and dominate the \nsynoptic pattern going into Wednesday, continuing the quiet and \nrelatively seasonable stretch. A dying cold front will come through \ntonight and early tomorrow morning and bring a few light showers, \nbut weak forcing and dry low levels will minimize the impacts. \nOverall, highs will be in the 70s and lows will be in the 40s and \n50s with low humidity.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A more dynamic fall-like synoptic pattern is beginning \nto become established. On Wednesday into Thursday, a deepening low \nwill move east from northern Ontario and into northern Quebec, with \nensemble guidance indicating it will likely drop to around 985 mb by \nthe time it reaches Atlantic Canada. The center of the low will pass \nby well to the north and the best dynamics should miss the region. \nWhile warm air advection will occur Tuesday and Wednesday and bring \na more humid airmass into the region, there will not be enough time \nfor a fully tropical airmass to become established. Therefore, this \nwill limit instability as the system passes by. The highest \ninstability will be across the St. Lawrence Valley where the warm \nairmass has the most time to become established. Dew points there \nshould briefly reach the mid 60s to around 70 and a few rumbles of \nthunder are possible there. Rain arrives for late Wednesday in \nnorthern areas as a warm front pushes through, though it appears to \nlack much elevated instability so the rain should just be \nstratiform. A strong southwesterly low-level jet will reach the 40-\n50 KT range at 850 mb, causing downsloping in the typical spots like \nthe Champlain Valley, limiting rainfall totals. It will also lead to \nwidespread gusts in the 20-30 mph range, with locally higher gusts. \nThe cold front passes through Wednesday night, but the warm sector \nnever looks to become fully established in the region, so most \nconvection should remain to the southwest. St. Lawrence County and \nsouthern Vermont look to be the places that may see more convective \nenhancement, while the rest of the region should mostly see \nsynoptically forced rain. Overall expected totals range in the 0.25 \nto 0.5 inch range for most places, with some higher totals locally \nacross northern New York.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: High pressure will dominate the weekend. The potency of \ncold air doesn't seem quite as strong as a couple days ago, but \nFriday will likely only reach the mid 60s and lower 70s. Then, with \nthe very dry air in place, temperatures are likely to take a nose \ndive Friday night. There may be a little return flow that gets \nstarted over northern New York, but otherwise unimpeded radiational \ncooling should bring temperatures into the 40s with a handful of \nmid/upper 30s in cold hollows except near Lake Champlain or the St. \nLawrence River where some lower 50s are more likely.\n\nHigh pressure will amble offshore as the weekend progresses.\nTemperatures will steadily climb to seasonal norms on Saturday, and \nthen above normal on Sunday as a stretched out cold front \napproaches. The frontal boundary may not push very far given minimal \nforcing and a stronger system developing over the northern Plains \nnext Sunday. So there doesn't seem to be much in the way of \nwidespread rain Sunday evening into next Monday when that front will \napproach.",
